WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:41.000
<v Priya Nair>Hi everyone, welcome in — we're just letting a few more folks trickle in from the waiting room, so give us thirty seconds. I can already see people joining from, looks like, six or seven different countries today, which is great. While we wait, drop in the chat where you're joining from, I always like to see that.

2
00:00:41.000 --> 00:01:15.000
<v Priya Nair>Okay, I think we're at a good number now, so let's get going, we've got a lot to cover in the next hour. My name is Priya Nair, I lead marketing here at ACME Revenue Cloud, and I will be your host and moderator today. Thank you all so much for carving out time — I know webinar fatigue is real, so the fact that you're here means a lot, and I promise we are going to make this worth the sixty-two minutes.

3
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:52.000
<v Priya Nair>Today's session is called "Pipeline After the Webinar: Turning Event Engagement into Revenue," and honestly the reason we built this session is kind of embarrassing — for years our own team ran webinars, got great attendance, felt good about ourselves, and then just... let the leads sit. Like, sit for days. So this is equal parts what we learned from doing it wrong and what actually works.

4
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:20.000
<v Priya Nair>I'm joined today by two people I've wanted to get on a stage, or a screen I guess, with for a long time. Daniel Kim, who runs demand gen over at Northwind Analytics, and Sara Alvarez, who leads RevOps at Meridian Software. Daniel, Sara, welcome — do you want to say a quick hello before we dive in?

5
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:48.000
<v Daniel Kim>Yeah, thanks Priya, happy to be here. Hi everyone. I run demand generation at Northwind Analytics — we're an analytics platform, mid-market mostly, and my whole world for the last three years has basically been fixing what happens in the seventy-two hours after somebody registers for or attends one of our events. So this topic is very close to home.

6
00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:10.000
<v Sara Alvarez>And hi from me too — Sara Alvarez, I lead revenue operations at Meridian Software. I sit kind of in between marketing and sales, so my job is honestly to be the annoying person who asks "okay but did that actually turn into pipeline" every time someone tells me a webinar went well. I promise I'm more fun than that sounds.

7
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:44.000
<v Priya Nair>[laughs] No that's — that's exactly the energy we need today. Okay, quick housekeeping — this is being recorded, we'll send it out to everyone including no-shows, so don't stress about taking notes on every slide. There's a Q&amp;A box, not the chat, please use the Q&amp;A box, we'll take live questions in the last stretch. Alright. Let's get into it.

8
00:03:44.000 --> 00:04:20.000
<v Priya Nair>So the framing for today — I want to walk through four things. First, speed-to-lead, basically why the clock starts the second the webinar ends and not a day later. Second, segmentation — because attendees, no-shows, and speakers are three completely different audiences and most teams still send one email to all of them. Third, content repurposing, and specifically whether it's actually worth the effort or just busywork. And fourth, attribution — how do you even prove any of this mattered.

9
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:30.000
<v Priya Nair>Before we get into the substance, let's do real introductions. Daniel, walk us through how you ended up owning this problem at Northwind.

10
00:04:30.000 --> 00:05:10.000
<v Daniel Kim>Sure. So I've been at Northwind about four years now, Head of Demand Gen for the last two. When I joined, we were running maybe two webinars a month, decent attendance, three hundred, four hundred registrants each, and then just... nothing happened. Leads went into a general nurture list, sales would maybe glance at them a week later, and by then honestly a lot of them had already moved on or forgotten they'd even registered. So my first real project was just — can we compress that window.

11
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:32.000
<v Daniel Kim>And what we found, and I'll share the number properly later, but the short version is follow-up within four hours lifts reply rate three point two x compared to next-day follow-up. Three point two x. That was the stat that got budget approved for the whole rebuild, honestly.

12
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:42.000
<v Priya Nair>We'll come back to that, I want Sara to weigh in on the operational side of what "four hours" even requires. Sara, your path into this?

13
00:05:42.000 --> 00:06:20.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Yeah so I came at this from the opposite direction — I was in sales ops before RevOps was really a title anyone used, and my complaint was always the reverse of what Daniel's describing. Marketing would hand sales a list of "engaged leads" and half the time sales had no idea what "engaged" meant. Did they attend the whole thing? Did they ask a question? Did they just register and never show up? Nobody knew. So attribution and lead scoring became my obsession because the handoff was just broken.

14
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:48.000
<v Sara Alvarez>At Meridian now we've rebuilt that whole pipe, and one thing that still surprises people when I say it out loud — accounts with three or more attendees on the same webinar convert to opportunity at almost double the rate of single-attendee accounts. Multi-threading basically happens by accident at a webinar and most teams don't even notice it's happening.

15
00:06:48.000 --> 00:08:10.000
<v Priya Nair>That's a great one to file away, we'll dig into that in the segmentation section. Okay — let's actually start with segment one, speed-to-lead. Daniel, take us through it.

16
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:35.000
<v Daniel Kim>Okay so, speed-to-lead. The core idea is dead simple and everybody nods along when you say it, but almost nobody's process actually reflects it: the value of a lead decays, like, aggressively, from the moment the event ends. It's not linear either, it's closer to a cliff.

17
00:08:35.000 --> 00:09:20.000
<v Daniel Kim>Here's the breakdown we've validated internally, across probably eleven, twelve webinars now. Follow-up sent within the first hour after the session ends — best reply rates we see, full stop. Within four hours, still strong, that's the three point two x number versus next-day I mentioned. By twenty-four hours, you've lost most of that lift, reply rate drops to something like one point four x versus next-day, and by forty-eight hours you're basically at baseline, like it barely matters anymore whether you send at hour forty-eight or hour ninety-six.

18
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.000
<v Priya Nair>So there's really a four-hour window where most of the value lives.

19
00:09:24.000 --> 00:10:00.000
<v Daniel Kim>Basically, yeah. And the reason is obvious once you say it out loud — the person just spent an hour thinking about your topic, it's fresh, they might still have the tab open. A day later they're back in their actual job, dealing with their actual fires, and your webinar is a distant memory competing with forty other emails in their inbox.

20
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:18.000
<v Priya Nair>Okay but here's my pushback, and I think a lot of people watching are thinking this too — that's easy to say when you've got one webinar and a small list. What happens at scale? You can't have a human write a personalized four-hour follow-up to eight hundred people.

21
00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:55.000
<v Daniel Kim>Right, and that's — honestly that's the whole unlock. You can't do it manually at scale, full stop, nobody's SDR team is sitting there at 6pm on a Tuesday hand-writing four hundred emails. What changed for us was using the transcript and the registration data together to generate the first draft automatically, segmented by role, and then a human just reviews and hits send rather than writes from scratch. That's the difference between "four hours" being aspirational and "four hours" being Tuesday.

22
00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:25.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Can I jump in here — because I think there's a nuance people miss, which is speed-to-lead isn't just about the thank-you email, it's about internal speed too. If marketing generates a lead score and that score sits in a queue for two days before an SDR even sees it, doesn't matter how fast the thank-you email went out, you've already lost the moment on the sales side.

23
00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:30.000
<v Priya Nair>So it's a two-sided clock — outbound comms and internal routing.

24
00:11:30.000 --> 00:12:05.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Exactly. And this is where I'll throw in a number from our side — when we cut the internal routing delay from an average of about eighteen hours down to under two hours, our webinar-sourced opportunity creation rate went up by forty-one percent over the following quarter. Same leads, same list quality, just faster internal handoff.

25
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.000
<v Daniel Kim>That's a huge number.

26
00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:28.000
<v Sara Alvarez>It is, and it's honestly the easiest fix on this whole call for most teams, because it's not even a content problem, it's a workflow problem. It's "does the lead score update trigger a task automatically or does someone have to remember to check a list."

27
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:40.000
<v Priya Nair>Okay so walk me through what "good" actually looks like operationally. Like hour by hour, what happens after the webinar ends.

28
00:12:40.000 --> 00:13:45.000
<v Daniel Kim>Sure, I'll do our version. Webinar ends at, say, 2pm. Within about fifteen minutes the platform exports attendance data — who showed up, when they joined, when they dropped, any poll or Q&amp;A activity. That feeds into a scoring model, nothing fancy, just weighted by engagement signals. By 2:45 we've got a segmented list — hot attendees, lukewarm attendees, no-shows, and speakers get pulled out separately obviously. By 3, generation of the actual follow-up content kicks off using the transcript for takeaways specific to what was actually said, not some generic template. By 4, a human — usually me or someone on my team — has reviewed and approved sends. Emails go out by 5, 6 at the latest. That's the four-hour window, roughly.

29
00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:50.000
<v Priya Nair>And the sales side, Sara, in that same window?

30
00:13:50.000 --> 00:14:30.000
<v Sara Alvarez>In parallel, the hot-attendee segment — so people who asked questions, stayed the full session, clicked pricing-related content before — those get pushed as tasks to the right rep based on territory and account ownership, ideally within that same first hour or two. Not a list dump at end of day. A task, assigned, with context, like "this person attended the full session and asked about integration timelines in the Q&amp;and A" — that context matters enormously for how the rep opens the conversation.

31
00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:42.000
<v Priya Nair>I want to poke at something — what about false positives? Like people who attended the whole thing because they had it open in another tab while doing something else.

32
00:14:42.000 --> 00:15:15.000
<v Daniel Kim>Yeah that's real, and honestly we don't fully solve it, nobody does. What we do is weight signals rather than treat attendance as binary. Did they answer a poll. Did they stay through the Q&amp;A specifically, which tends to filter out the "background tab" crowd because people doing something else usually drop before Q&amp;A. Did they click anything in chat. It's imperfect but it's way better than "attended yes or no."

33
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:35.000
<v Sara Alvarez>And to be fair, even an imperfect score beats no score. The alternative most teams have is literally alphabetical order or whoever registered first gets called first, which has zero correlation with actual interest.

34
00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:44.000
<v Priya Nair>Let's talk about the no-show segment for a second because I think people assume no-shows are just... dead leads.

35
00:15:44.000 --> 00:16:20.000
<v Daniel Kim>Huge mistake if you think that. No-shows are often just as valuable, sometimes more, because they self-selected into your topic enough to register, then something legitimate came up. Calendar conflict, higher priority meeting, whatever. Our no-show follow-up — recording link plus a genuinely useful three-bullet summary, not "sorry we missed you" fluff — that email actually gets a better click-through rate than our attendee follow-up in like six of the last eight webinars. Six of eight.

36
00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:23.000
<v Priya Nair>Wait, better than attendees?

37
00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:45.000
<v Daniel Kim>Better than attendees, yeah, which surprised us the first time we saw it too. Our working theory is no-shows have higher intent-to-consume because they haven't gotten the content yet, whereas some attendees feel like they already got the value and don't click through again.

38
00:16:45.000 --> 00:17:15.000
<v Sara Alvarez>That matches what we see too. I'd add — don't just send the recording to no-shows, send them the recording with a timestamp link to the most relevant five minutes for their apparent interest, if you have that data from their registration form. "You mentioned integration challenges when you registered, here's the twelve-minute mark where Daniel covers exactly that." Way higher engagement than "here's the whole hour, good luck."

39
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:28.000
<v Priya Nair>Okay let's talk about the actual copy for a second, because I think teams get the timing right sometimes but still write a bad email. What's changed in how you write these?

40
00:17:28.000 --> 00:18:05.000
<v Daniel Kim>The biggest shift for us was stopping writing one email and starting writing, like, four or five subject line variants and two or three body variants per segment, and actually testing them rather than guessing. Sounds obvious but most teams write one email because writing five by hand for every single webinar is exhausting, nobody has the bandwidth. That's really only become practical once generation got cheap and fast — a human can review five drafts in the time it used to take to write one from scratch.

41
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:15.000
<v Priya Nair>Do you have a sense of how much subject line actually moves the needle versus, I don't know, being a rounding error?

42
00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:50.000
<v Daniel Kim>It's not a rounding error. On our last big webinar we A/B tested two subject lines on the attendee thank-you send — one that led with the speaker's name, one that led with a specific stat from the session — and the stat-led subject line got a fourteen-point-something percent higher open rate. I don't want to overstate subject lines, timing still matters more, but it's not nothing.

43
00:18:50.000 --> 00:19:00.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Can we talk about the speaker thank-you email for a second, because I think it's the most skipped one and it shouldn't be.

44
00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.000
<v Priya Nair>Please, yes, because we are historically terrible at this one internally.

45
00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:52.000
<v Sara Alvarez>[laughs] A lot of teams are. The speaker thank-you — especially when it's an external speaker like Daniel today, or like me — should not just be a generic "thanks for joining." It should include an actual performance snapshot. How many people attended, what the engagement looked like during their specific segment, maybe a top quote of theirs that resonated, any pipeline that's traceable back to the session so far. That does two things — it's genuinely nice to receive, and it's also how you get invited back, or how you get that speaker to reshare the recording to their own network, which is free distribution you're leaving on the table if the thank-you is generic.

46
00:19:52.000 --> 00:20:20.000
<v Daniel Kim>I'll vouch for that from the receiving end — I've spoken at maybe twenty external webinars over the years and I can count on one hand how many sent me anything beyond "thanks for your time!" The ones that sent me a real snapshot, I reshared every single one of those to my own LinkedIn without being asked.

47
00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:45.000
<v Priya Nair>That's a great plug for segment two actually, because that's exactly the segmentation point — attendee, no-show, and speaker are fundamentally different audiences with different needs, and yet I bet most people on this call today, no judgment, are sending one email to everybody.

48
00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:58.000
<v Priya Nair>Before we move on, small tangent, because someone in the chat is asking about internal speakers specifically — do we, ACME, actually do this well for our own team? Because I want to be honest here.

49
00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:02.000
<v Daniel Kim>[laughs] Put on the spot.

50
00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:35.000
<v Priya Nair>No, genuinely, I'll self-report — we historically did not send our own internal speakers any kind of performance snapshot at all, because the assumption internally was "well, they already know how it went, they were there." Which, in hindsight, is a ridiculous assumption. Being in the room doesn't tell you the click-through rate on your quote three weeks later.

51
00:21:35.000 --> 00:22:10.000
<v Sara Alvarez>That's actually a really common blind spot — teams remember to do the relationship-maintenance version of the speaker thank-you for external guests because there's an obvious incentive, you want them back. Internal speakers get skipped because there's no obvious relationship risk if you don't. But internal speakers are the ones who'll fight for budget for the next event, and a good snapshot is basically free ammunition for them to go tell their own leadership the session mattered.

52
00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:28.000
<v Daniel Kim>Agreed, and honestly it's a cheap fix once you're already generating the snapshot for external speakers anyway — it's the same pipeline, you're just not skipping a row.

53
00:22:28.000 --> 00:24:05.000
<v Priya Nair>Noted, and mildly embarrassing to admit live, but that's the spirit of today's session I think, so I'll leave it in.

54
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:18.000
<v Priya Nair>Okay let's move into segment two properly — segmentation. Sara, you flagged that multi-threading stat earlier, walk us through segmentation more broadly.

55
00:24:18.000 --> 00:25:00.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Sure. So the way I think about post-event segmentation, there's really three layers. Layer one is the coarse split — attendee versus no-show versus speaker, which we already covered. Layer two is role and seniority — a VP who attended needs different content than an individual contributor who attended, even if they saw the exact same session. Layer three, and this is the one people skip most, is account-level context — not just who this person is, but who else at their company engaged with this event or with you generally.

56
00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.000
<v Priya Nair>And that's where the multi-threading stat comes from.

57
00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:38.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Right — the number I mentioned earlier, accounts with three-plus attendees convert to opportunity at nearly double the rate of single-attendee accounts. So if you're not looking at the account level, you might treat three attendees from the same company as three separate leads to route separately, when actually the right move might be a single coordinated outreach that acknowledges "hey, we noticed a few folks from your team joined."

58
00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:44.000
<v Priya Nair>Do you actually say that in the email? "We noticed three people from your company attended"?

59
00:25:44.000 --> 00:26:15.000
<v Sara Alvarez>We've tested it both ways honestly. Saying it explicitly in an email can feel slightly surveillance-y if it's worded wrong, so we tend to save that observation for the sales rep's outreach rather than the automated email — "hey, I saw a few folks from your team on the session Tuesday, curious what's driving the interest right now" lands much better coming from a human in a personalized message than from an automated marketing email.

60
00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:45.000
<v Daniel Kim>We do something similar, and I'd add a fourth layer honestly, which is industry and company size, because the takeaways we pull out of the same transcript literally change based on who's reading them. A five hundred person company cares about a different part of the same webinar than a twenty person startup does, even if they watched the identical recording.

61
00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:52.000
<v Priya Nair>Can you make that concrete? Like what would actually be different in the email.

62
00:26:52.000 --> 00:27:25.000
<v Daniel Kim>Sure — say the session covers pricing tiers and integration depth. For an enterprise attendee, the takeaway snippet we surface leads with integration and security, because that's usually the blocker at that size. For a smaller company, we lead with time-to-value and pricing, because that's usually the actual objection. Same transcript, same event, genuinely different three-sentence summary depending on who's opening the email.

63
00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:40.000
<v Priya Nair>That's a lot of variants to manage though. Like if you've got attendee times no-show times speaker times seniority times company size, that's — that's a lot of emails.

64
00:27:40.000 --> 00:28:10.000
<v Sara Alvarez>It is, and honestly that's exactly why this has to be systematized rather than hand-written, because manually you'd cap out at maybe three variants before a human just gives up and sends the generic one. The moment you can generate variant drafts fast and cheap, and a human just reviews rather than authors from scratch, the ceiling on how granular you can get goes way up.

65
00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:20.000
<v Priya Nair>Let's talk numbers on segmentation specifically — do you have anything on lift from segmented versus blanket sends?

66
00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:52.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Yeah, on our side, when we moved from a single blanket post-webinar email to the three-way segmentation, attendee, no-show, speaker, with role-based variants layered in after that, our overall click-through across the full post-event send went from around six percent to just over seventeen percent. That's not a small bump, that's basically triple.

67
00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:56.000
<v Priya Nair>Triple, from segmentation alone, without changing timing.

68
00:28:56.000 --> 00:29:10.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Without changing timing at all in that test, yeah — timing was actually held constant specifically so we could isolate the segmentation variable.

69
00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:35.000
<v Daniel Kim>I'd caveat slightly — in our data timing and segmentation compound, they're not fully independent, doing both well beats doing either well in isolation by more than the sum of the parts, in our experience. But yes, segmentation alone moves a lot.

70
00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:42.000
<v Priya Nair>What about attendees who dropped off partway through? Is that its own segment?

71
00:29:42.000 --> 00:30:15.000
<v Daniel Kim>It should be, and most teams don't bother, but it's a meaningfully different signal. Someone who dropped at minute eight probably realized this wasn't the session for them. Someone who dropped at minute fifty probably got pulled into a meeting and might've genuinely wanted the rest. We treat late-drop as closer to a "hot" signal, actually — send them the recording with a note pointing to what they missed after their drop point.

72
00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:20.000
<v Priya Nair>That's such a small detail but it makes total sense once you say it.

73
00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:40.000
<v Sara Alvarez>A lot of this is small details, honestly, that's kind of the theme of the whole talk. None of these individually feel revolutionary, it's the compounding of a dozen small correct decisions instead of a dozen small default ones.

74
00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:52.000
<v Priya Nair>Let's talk speaker segmentation for one more second, because I don't think we've fully covered — do you segment your speaker thank-yous by internal versus external speaker?

75
00:30:52.000 --> 00:31:30.000
<v Sara Alvarez>We do, yes. Internal speakers, like say Priya today, that's more of an internal recognition and metrics loop, less about relationship maintenance because obviously she works here. External speakers, Daniel today for example, that's much more relationship maintenance — you genuinely want that person to want to do this again, and to talk about you positively to their own network. So the tone and the content of those two thank-yous should be pretty different even though both are technically "speaker thank-you" emails.

76
00:31:30.000 --> 00:36:05.000
<v Priya Nair>Makes sense. Okay, I want to pause segmentation there because I think we've covered the core of it, and move into content repurposing, because I know a lot of people registered specifically for this part.

77
00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:28.000
<v Priya Nair>Alright, segment three — content repurposing. And I want to be honest about my own skepticism here, because for a long time I thought repurposing a sixty-minute webinar into ten different assets was just busywork that made a marketing team look productive without actually driving anything. Daniel, change my mind.

78
00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:55.000
<v Daniel Kim>[laughs] Fair skepticism, and honestly it used to be busywork at Northwind too, because the way we did it originally was exactly the failure mode you're describing — an intern would watch the whole recording, pull some quotes manually, write a blog post, and it would take like a week and a half, by which point the moment had completely passed and nobody cared anymore.

79
00:36:55.000 --> 00:37:30.000
<v Daniel Kim>What changed the ROI calculation for us wasn't the idea of repurposing, it was the speed and the source. Working directly off the transcript rather than someone's memory of watching the video means you catch things a tired intern misses at minute fifty of a rewatch. And doing it within like forty-eight hours instead of two weeks means the content is still riding the wave of the actual event instead of feeling stale.

80
00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:40.000
<v Priya Nair>Give me the actual output list, like what does "repurposing one webinar" produce concretely for you.

81
00:37:40.000 --> 00:38:35.000
<v Daniel Kim>For a typical sixty-minute session at Northwind: one blog post, eight hundred to twelve hundred words, built around the core argument of the session plus the best two or three quotes. YouTube chapter markers so people can jump to the part they care about instead of scrubbing blindly, plus a description and a thumbnail brief. An infographic outline built around the data points mentioned in the session — and there's usually more of those buried in a transcript than anyone remembers, because in the room you're following the argument, not counting stats. And then somewhere between eight and ten social posts split across LinkedIn and what used to be Twitter, each with a genuinely different hook rather than the same sentence chopped ten ways.

82
00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:42.000
<v Priya Nair>And the ROI question — do you actually have a number on this or is it a vibe?

83
00:38:42.000 --> 00:39:30.000
<v Daniel Kim>We have a number, and it convinced our CFO, which is a higher bar than convincing me. Repurposed content from a single sixty-minute webinar — across the blog post, the social posts, and the infographic — generated, on average across our last six events, about two point four times the pipeline-influenced touches of the original live session itself. The live session is one touch with maybe four hundred people. The repurposed content, over the following month, touches a much wider audience who never even registered for the original event, several times over.

84
00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:38.000
<v Priya Nair>Two point four x the touches of the live event, from stuff that's essentially a byproduct of an event you were already running.

85
00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:55.000
<v Daniel Kim>Right, that's the framing that got budget, exactly that — this isn't new content spend, it's yield on content you already produced and mostly let evaporate after the live hour ended.

86
00:39:55.000 --> 00:40:25.000
<v Sara Alvarez>I'll add the RevOps angle on this, which is slightly different — the thing I care about is whether repurposed content actually gets tagged and tracked properly, because I've seen teams do beautiful repurposing work and then have zero idea whether any of it touched a closed-won deal, because nobody set up the tracking. Repurposing without attribution is just content marketing theater.

87
00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:38.000
<v Priya Nair>Which is a perfect segue but let's stay on repurposing for one more beat — what about the infographic specifically, because that one feels the hardest to automate well.

88
00:40:38.000 --> 00:41:20.000
<v Daniel Kim>It's the one that needs the most human polish at the end, agreed, but the outline — which data points to feature, in what order, with what framing — that part comes almost entirely out of a good transcript pass. A sixty-minute session usually has, in our experience, somewhere between six and ten genuinely citable data points buried in the conversation that nobody would remember without going back through it carefully. Pulling those out and ranking them by how surprising or specific they are, that's the unlock, then a designer just needs a day, not a week, to make it look good.

89
00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:32.000
<v Priya Nair>Sara, from the RevOps seat, what do you actually require before repurposed content gets published, in terms of tracking?

90
00:41:32.000 --> 00:42:05.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Every single asset needs its own UTM, no exceptions, tied back to the original event in our CRM as a campaign. Blog post, each individual social post, the infographic, the YouTube description link — each one distinct, so that three months from now when a deal closes I can actually tell you it touched, say, the LinkedIn post with Sara's quote about multi-threading, not just "something webinar-related, probably."

91
00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:12.000
<v Priya Nair>That level of granularity — is that overkill for most teams starting out?

92
00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:40.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Honestly, no, and I'd push back gently on "overkill" — UTMs are basically free to set up if you build the convention once and template it. The overkill is doing the repurposing work and skipping the two minutes of tagging, because then you've done the hard part and thrown away your ability to prove it mattered.

93
00:42:40.000 --> 00:43:10.000
<v Daniel Kim>Strongly agree, and I'd add — tag by event, not just by campaign type, from day one. We didn't do that early on, tagged everything as generic "webinar-content," and by the time we wanted to compare event A's repurposing performance against event B's, the data was mush. Learned that one the hard way.

94
00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:22.000
<v Priya Nair>Let's talk about the social posts specifically, since that's usually the highest volume output. How do you keep eight to ten posts from all sounding the same?

95
00:43:22.000 --> 00:44:15.000
<v Daniel Kim>The trick is picking a genuinely different angle for each one rather than a different sentence. One post might be the single boldest quote from the session, standalone, no context, just the quote and the speaker's name — those tend to perform really well actually, people underestimate the pure quote post. Another might be a contrarian take one of the speakers made. Another might be a data point framed as a question — "is your team still doing X" — that invites comments. Another might be a behind-the-scenes note about a question from the audience that surprised the panel. If you try to summarize the whole event in every post you get ten posts that all feel like the same press release.

96
00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:40.000
<v Sara Alvarez>From a numbers standpoint, we've found the quote posts, exactly like Daniel's describing, get around forty percent higher engagement than the summary-style posts in our own testing. People respond to a specific sharp sentence way more than a recap.

97
00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:52.000
<v Priya Nair>That's a great stat to close this segment on actually — quote posts outperforming summary posts by that much.

98
00:44:52.000 --> 00:45:05.000
<v Priya Nair>One more thing before we move on, slight tangent — YouTube chapters specifically, does anyone actually use those, or is that effort wasted?

99
00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:40.000
<v Daniel Kim>I was skeptical too, honestly, felt like a nice-to-have nobody clicks. But we started tracking it and chapter markers meaningfully increase average watch time on the recording, because people jump to the part relevant to them instead of bouncing in the first ninety seconds when it's not immediately clear the thing they care about is coming later. It's a small lift to build once you've got the transcript segmented anyway, so the marginal cost is basically zero.

100
00:45:40.000 --> 00:46:05.000
<v Sara Alvarez>And from a tracking standpoint, chapter clicks are a decent proxy signal too — if someone jumps straight to the pricing chapter and lingers there, that's a data point worth feeding back into their lead score, the same way a pricing-page visit on your website would be.

101
00:46:05.000 --> 00:46:20.000
<v Priya Nair>That's a good point, treating video engagement with the same rigor as web analytics rather than as a separate, vaguer bucket.

102
00:46:20.000 --> 00:46:38.000
<v Daniel Kim>Right, and most teams do treat it as separate, mostly because the tooling historically made it annoying to connect the two. That's changing fast though.

103
00:46:38.000 --> 00:48:05.000
<v Priya Nair>Okay, let's move into attribution, because I think that ties everything we've discussed together and is honestly the hardest part.

104
00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:14.000
<v Priya Nair>Segment four, attribution. Sara, this is squarely your domain, so take the wheel.

105
00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:50.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Happy to. So attribution, for the post-event context specifically, I think about in three layers, similar structure to the segmentation piece actually. Layer one — did the person become a marketing qualified lead, and how fast, after the event. Layer two — did that MQL become a sales accepted lead and eventually an opportunity, and how much of that speed traces back to the event versus other touches. Layer three — did it close, and what multiple of the event's cost did it return.

106
00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:56.000
<v Priya Nair>Let's do layer one first — the speed from event to MQL.

107
00:48:56.000 --> 00:49:35.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Sure. Contacts who attended a webinar and got timely, well-segmented follow-up convert to MQL at meaningfully higher rates than contacts who just registered and got left in general nurture. In our data specifically, attendees who received segmented follow-up within that critical window reach MQL status about sixty percent faster, measured in days, than attendees who got a generic delayed follow-up. Sixty percent faster time-to-MQL.

108
00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:46.000
<v Priya Nair>Sixty percent faster — is that mostly the timing effect we discussed in segment one, or the segmentation effect from segment two, or both compounding?

109
00:49:46.000 --> 00:50:10.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Genuinely both, and honestly separating them cleanly is hard because in practice teams that nail timing also tend to be the teams that nail segmentation — it's rare to see one without the other, they're both symptoms of a team that's actually operationalized this instead of treating it as an afterthought.

110
00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:40.000
<v Daniel Kim>I'd add a wrinkle here — attribution gets messy the moment a lead touches more than one asset, which happens constantly. Someone gets the thank-you email, then later clicks a repurposed social post, then reads the blog post two weeks after that. First-touch and last-touch models will tell you wildly different stories about what actually mattered.

111
00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:50.000
<v Priya Nair>How do you handle that practically? Not everyone on this call has a full multi-touch attribution model built out.

112
00:50:50.000 --> 00:51:35.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Totally fair, and you don't need a perfect model to start. What I'd tell anyone starting from scratch — at minimum, tag the original event as a campaign in your CRM, and make sure every single downstream asset, the emails, the repurposed content, everything, rolls up to that same campaign ID. Even without a sophisticated multi-touch weighting model, you can then at least answer "did anything from this event touch this closed deal," which is layer one of attribution and honestly answers eighty percent of the questions leadership actually asks.

113
00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:48.000
<v Priya Nair>Let's talk actual dollars, if you're comfortable sharing. What's the return you've actually measured on an event, all-in.

114
00:51:48.000 --> 00:52:30.000
<v Sara Alvarez>I'll give you a directional number rather than a specific dollar figure since it varies a lot by deal size, but — across the events we've been able to fully attribute end to end at Meridian, when you account for the original event cost plus the follow-up and repurposing effort, we're seeing something in the range of eleven to fourteen x return on fully-loaded event cost within two quarters, once you count both the direct pipeline from attendees and the secondary pipeline sourced through repurposed content touching people who never even attended live.

115
00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:38.000
<v Priya Nair>Eleven to fourteen x, within two quarters. That's a big number to say out loud to a CFO.

116
00:52:38.000 --> 00:53:15.000
<v Sara Alvarez>It is, and I'd genuinely caution people not to just repeat that number without doing their own math, because it depends heavily on deal size and sales cycle length in your specific business — a company with a six-month enterprise cycle isn't going to see the same shape of return in the same two-quarter window that we do. But directionally, when you actually chase down all three layers instead of stopping at "we got some MQLs," the number is almost always bigger than what people assumed going in.

117
00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:45.000
<v Daniel Kim>The thing I'd underline from the demand gen side is — none of that return shows up if layer one and two, the speed and segmentation stuff, are broken. Attribution doesn't create value, it just measures value that was created by doing the earlier steps well. You can have a perfect attribution model and still get a bad number if your follow-up went out three days late to an unsegmented list.

118
00:53:45.000 --> 00:54:15.000
<v Priya Nair>That's a good note to bring us toward the close of the core content — the four pieces genuinely build on each other, speed enables segmentation to matter, segmentation and speed together drive the MQL lift, and attribution is just how you prove it happened rather than a lever on its own.

119
00:54:15.000 --> 00:54:22.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Exactly right. Attribution is the mirror, not the engine.

120
00:54:22.000 --> 00:54:35.000
<v Priya Nair>"Attribution is the mirror, not the engine" — I'm stealing that, that's going on a slide somewhere.

121
00:54:35.000 --> 00:54:50.000
<v Priya Nair>Quick tangent before Q&amp;A — Sara, does that eleven-to-fourteen-x figure hold up the same way for a first-time webinar host, or is that a mature-program number that took years to get to?

122
00:54:50.000 --> 00:55:20.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Fair challenge, and no, I wouldn't set that as a first-event expectation. That number reflects a program we've iterated on for a while — better segmentation, faster routing, tighter attribution tagging all compounding together. A first webinar with none of that infrastructure in place is going to see a much smaller multiple, and that's fine, that's normal. The point isn't "expect this number immediately," it's "this is the ceiling once the underlying process is actually built well, so it's worth building toward."

123
00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:45.000
<v Daniel Kim>Agreed, and I'd say the honest first-event expectation is closer to just proving the pipeline touched anything measurable at all — get that muscle built, the multiple grows as the process matures, not the other way around.

124
00:55:45.000 --> 00:56:15.000
<v Priya Nair>Okay, we've got a good number of questions queued up in the Q&amp;A box, let's get through as many as we can. First one — this is from someone who joined from, looks like, a mid-market SaaS company — "We're a five-person marketing team, no dedicated ops person. Where do we even start with all of this?"

125
00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:52.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Great question and a really common starting point honestly. If I had to pick one thing to fix first with zero budget and no dedicated ops person, it's the internal routing speed we talked about earlier — getting hot leads to a human faster, even manually, even with something as simple as a shared spreadsheet checked twice a day instead of once a week. That single change, cutting routing delay, moved our own numbers more than almost anything else we tried, and it costs nothing but a bit of discipline.

126
00:56:52.000 --> 00:57:25.000
<v Daniel Kim>I'd add — pick one segment to do well before trying to do all three. Most teams that fail at this try to build the full attendee-no-show-speaker system on day one and burn out. Just nail the attendee thank-you with real transcript-based takeaways first, get that habit in place, then add the no-show segment next month, then speaker thank-yous after that.

127
00:57:25.000 --> 00:57:38.000
<v Priya Nair>Next question — "How do you handle GDPR or data privacy concerns when personalizing based on registration data?"

128
00:57:38.000 --> 00:58:15.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Important question. The short answer is — whatever personalization you're doing should only use data the person actually gave you consent for at registration, and it should be pretty obviously in-context, not creepy. "You mentioned integration challenges" based on a form field they filled in themselves is fine. Anything that feels like it's inferring things they didn't tell you, that's where you want to be much more careful, and honestly where a human review step before send matters most, not just for tone but for exactly this kind of judgment call.

129
00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:28.000
<v Priya Nair>Third question — "What tools are you both using for the transcript-to-content pipeline specifically?"

130
00:58:28.000 --> 00:59:05.000
<v Daniel Kim>We use a transcription step off the recording, then an LLM pass over that transcript to pull out quotes, data points, and chapter-worthy moments, feeding into templated outputs for blog, social, and the infographic outline. The important part isn't really the specific tool, it's that a human reviews every generated draft before it goes anywhere, we've never fully auto-published anything without a person looking at it first. AI drafts, human approves, every single time.

131
00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:18.000
<v Priya Nair>Last question, and I think it's a good one to end on — "What's the single biggest mistake you see teams make in this whole process?"

132
00:59:18.000 --> 00:59:50.000
<v Daniel Kim>For me it's treating every attendee identically. One email, one send time, regardless of whether they stayed the full hour or dropped at minute six. That single failure to segment quietly costs more pipeline than almost anything else on this list, and it's usually not a technology gap, it's just nobody decided to fix it.

133
00:59:50.000 --> 01:00:20.000
<v Sara Alvarez>Mine's closely related — doing the work of timing and segmentation well, and then not tagging any of it, so three months later nobody can prove any of it mattered and the whole program gets deprioritized in the next budget cycle for something that looks flashier but actually performs worse. Tag everything, from day one, even if the tracking feels tedious in the moment.

134
01:00:20.000 --> 01:01:10.000
<v Priya Nair>"Tag everything from day one" — noted, and honestly a fitting closing line.

135
01:01:10.000 --> 01:01:32.000
<v Priya Nair>Alright, we're basically at time, so let me close us out. Huge thank you to Daniel Kim from Northwind Analytics and Sara Alvarez from Meridian Software for genuinely one of the more concrete, number-heavy sessions we've run — no fluff, which I appreciate as the person who has to sit through a lot of these.

136
01:01:32.000 --> 01:01:58.000
<v Priya Nair>For everyone watching, live or on the recording later — the full recording, a written summary of the key data points we covered today, and a couple of follow-up resources will be in your inbox within the next few hours, not next week, which I promise is deliberate given everything we just talked about. If you have follow-up questions for Daniel or Sara, their details will be in that same email, and both have kindly agreed to take a few direct questions.

137
01:01:58.000 --> 01:02:00.000
<v Priya Nair>Thanks again everyone for your time today, we'll see you at the next one.

138
01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:20.000
<v SYSTEM>[Session ends]
